---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { base, issuesUrl, repoUrl } from '../site';

const title = 'Privacy policy · XMarks';
const description = 'What the XMarks extension stores, where it keeps it, and the few times it touches the network.';
const updated = '20 October 2026';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="privacy/">
  <article class="policy container">
    <header>
      <h1 class="section-title">Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lede">
      XMarks keeps your bookmarks in your browser. There is no XMarks account and no XMarks server, so we never receive
      your bookmarks, notes or anything else about you. This page lists what the extension stores, where, and the few times it
      uses the network.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What XMarks stores on your device</h2>
      <p>Your library is kept in your browser’s IndexedDB storage, on your device only:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The posts you save: their text, the author’s name and handle, the date, links in the post, and the web addresses of
          the author’s avatar and any images or videos. The images themselves are not copied.
        </li>
        <li>What you add: categories, tags and notes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Your settings (save mode, where the save button sits, accent color, theme, card density, and whether auto-categorize is
        on) are kept in Chrome’s extension storage. If you are signed in to Chrome with sync turned on, Chrome copies these
        settings between your browsers through your Google account, as it does for any extension. Your bookmarks and notes are
        not synced.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you add more than one library, their names and which one new saves go to are kept in extension storage on this
        device only. With “Follow the X account I’m logged into” turned on, each library also stores the handle of the X
        account it belongs to.
      </p>
      <p>XMarks also remembers which onboarding tips you have seen, on this device only.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When XMarks uses the network</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Images from X.</strong> The dashboard shows avatars, images and videos by loading them from X’s servers,
          the same way X does when you view the post. X can see that request, as it would if you opened the post on X.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The auto-categorize model.</strong> Auto-categorize is off until you turn it on. The first time you do, XMarks
          downloads a model (about 45 MB) from Hugging Face (huggingface.co) and your browser caches it. That download
          contains none of your bookmarks; Hugging Face sees an ordinary file request from your IP address. After that,
          categorizing runs entirely on your device.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Links you open.</strong> “View tweet” and other links open the post on X in a new tab.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Nothing else is sent anywhere. XMarks has no analytics, no tracking, no ads and no crash reporting.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What XMarks does on X</h2>
      <p>
        The extension only runs on x.com and twitter.com. There it adds the save button to posts, and reads a post’s content
        from the page when you save it. When you click Sync Bookmarks, it scrolls your Bookmarks list on X and saves each post
        it finds. It also reads the handle of the account you are logged into from X’s sidebar, so a save can go to that
        account’s library. It does not read your messages, change your account, or post anything for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>storage, unlimitedStorage</dt>
        <dd>To keep your library and settings in your browser, however large the library gets.</dd>
        <dt>Access to x.com and twitter.com</dt>
        <dd>To add the save button and Sync Bookmarks to X, and to read the posts you choose to save.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your control</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Export your whole library, or selected posts, to a JSON file at any time from the dashboard.</li>
        <li>Delete any post, category or note in the dashboard.</li>
        <li>Removing the extension deletes everything it stored in your browser. Export first if you want to keep a copy.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We don’t sell, share or transfer your data, because we never have it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is hosted on GitHub Pages. It uses no cookies and no analytics. If you pick an accent color, your browser
        remembers it for this site. GitHub may log visitors’ IP addresses for security; see
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub’s privacy statement</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the new version will be posted here with a new date. The extension is open source, so you can
        check all of this in <a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the code on GitHub</a>. Questions?
        <a href={issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open an issue</a>. The rules for using XMarks are in the
        <a href={`${base}terms/`}>terms of use</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 760px;
    padding-top: 48px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 40px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .updated {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .policy .lede {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: var(--track-m);
  }

  p,
  ul,
  dl {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 22px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  strong,
  dt {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  dd {
    margin: 2px 0 14px;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .policy {
      padding-top: 24px;
    }
  }
</style>
